WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.301
GGUF and llama.

2
00:00:01.581 --> 00:00:02.584
cpp.

3
00:00:02.864 --> 00:00:03.632
llama.

4
00:00:03.912 --> 00:00:19.123
cpp is a C and C++ inference engine that runs on CPUs and GPUs with few dependencies; its README calls Apple silicon a first-class citizen, optimized through ARM NEON, Accelerate, and Metal.

5
00:00:19.403 --> 00:00:25.675
It introduced GGUF, a single-file binary format designed for fast loading and memory mapping.

6
00:00:25.955 --> 00:00:35.128
A GGUF file holds the tensors plus key-value metadata: architecture, context length, tokenizer, chat template, and quantization type.

7
00:00:35.408 --> 00:00:43.365
That is why one GGUF file is enough to run a model, unlike a transformers repo with separate config and tokenizer files.

8
00:00:43.645 --> 00:00:50.493
File names follow a convention that encodes the base name, size, fine-tune, version, and quantization, so.

9
00:00:50.773 --> 00:00:53.163
-8B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.

10
00:00:53.443 --> 00:00:55.085
gguf tells you what you have.

11
00:00:55.365 --> 00:01:09.019
You get GGUF files in two ways: download them from the Hub (many popular models have official or community GGUF repos, linked through base_model ), or convert a transformers model and quantize it with llama-quantize,

12
00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:14.717
optionally using an importance matrix computed on sample text to reduce quality loss.

13
00:01:14.997 --> 00:01:26.368
llama-server serves a model over HTTP with OpenAI-compatible endpoints such as /v1/chat/completions and /v1/models.

14
00:01:26.648 --> 00:01:32.685
Note a recent change: the server README now gives the default address as 127.

15
00:01:32.965 --> 00:01:33.733
0.

16
00:01:34.013 --> 00:01:34.781
0.

17
00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:46.453
1 on port 9931, changed in October 2026 from the long-standing 8080, so older tutorials will point you at the wrong port.

18
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:48.973
Check the README or pass --port yourself.
